WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.920
Bernie Sanders who is a Senator running for
the 2020 democratic party press

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.150
essential nomination has published his immigration
plan and it's a good one.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:15.769
It's comprehensive and it deals with the major
issues that are surrounding immigration.

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:20.039
And one of the most important things about
this plan, which we're going to look at today

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:27.320
in some detail, is that there's the policy
stuff that is immediately related to immigration,

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:34.040
DACA, a child separations, but it's a plan
that is comprehensive in that it deals with

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:39.290
the issue as it exists in the real world,
which is connected to so many other issues.

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:45.140
And this is one of the things that there's
like a myopia that often takes place in politics

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:50.371
around controversial issues where each side,
and I'm not playing a both sides game, but

00:00:50.371 --> 00:00:53.120
both sides do this to some degree.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:00.160
Each side sort of buckles down in arguing
in a narrow way about particular elements

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:07.860
of a policy issue without considering in a
serious way the circumstances and the context

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:08.860
in which it exists.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:15.010
And when you look at something like immigration,
immigration relates to education, law enforcement,

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:19.740
healthcare, trade economics, all of these
other things that if you really want to deal

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:25.520
with it in a serious adult way, you have to
consider, and Bernie Sanders plan does that.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:26.560
So this'll be good.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.390
It's good to sometimes step back from the
polls and the tweets.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:34.610
This is Bernie's immigration plan and some
of the components that are included.

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:41.830
The right likes to talk about deportations
and the wall crossing the border is criminal

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:46.730
detain people, charge them, get them out,
doesn't deal with the circumstances and the

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:49.150
conditions that got us to where we are today.

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.300
And that's one of the best things about this
plan.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.260
So, uh, Bernie Sanders would end child separations
at the border.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:56.260
Okay.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:59.680
That's a specific action that can be taken
of via a directive.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.660
Uh, that's a good start.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.910
Bernie would reunite, separated children with
families.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.659
Make sense to do.

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:11.940
Doesn't mean people are not going to be held
accountable if they've committed crimes.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.260
It's not a opening the door to lawlessness
like the right likes to suggest.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.500
It just makes sense to do it.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.840
Auditing and closing detention centers.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.820
You don't necessarily need to close all of
them.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:27.819
And Bernie Sanders is not claiming that he
would, he would audit the entire system.

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:30.950
He would look at the conditions that are taking
place within individual detention centers

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:36.450
and he would figure out which ones need to
be reformed and which ones need to be closed.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.480
Very, Bernie would put a moratorium on all
deportations.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:48.600
Now this is a controversial element here because
even among many on the left in the democratic

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:56.990
party, uh, within the house and Senate, there
are not that many people who say, stop all

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:58.170
deportations.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:02.349
But what Bernie Sanders is saying here is
at least as we sort out some of these other

00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:08.860
elements, let's put a moratorium on deportations
because they're, uh, th th once you've rung

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:13.680
that bell, once the genie is out of the bottle,
once we've deported someone, um, the ability

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.090
to undo that if we decide that the policy
is changing is extremely limited, stopping

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.920
all construction of the border wall.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:20.920
Obvious.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:24.090
Now you might say, I didn't think that the
border wall was even under construction.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:30.099
Um, so we've got elements of the wall that
are being replaced with Donald Trump's version

00:03:30.099 --> 00:03:32.140
of the wall that were scheduled for replacement.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:37.260
As we know, you can either climb over them
as some have done, you can get $100 saw at

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.770
any hardware store and saw through it.

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:44.569
So it's not a, a virtually impenetrable fence
has Donald Trump has claimed it to be.

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:47.980
But Bernie is saying here, just cut and dry,
whatever it is that's going on.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:51.640
If and when he becomes president in terms
of construction of a border wall, we're going

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:52.950
to stop that.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.270
And overturning the Muslim ban as well.

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:58.090
Of course, Trump is say it's not really a
Muslim ban.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:03:59.970
Uh, we've had that debate.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:01.800
That is a policy that doesn't make any sense.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:05.300
It's a policy that doesn't make sense on the
merits because it's targeting countries that

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:11.959
have not actually been the sources of people,
uh, who come to the United States and do things,

00:04:11.959 --> 00:04:13.590
do violent things, commit terrorist acts.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.730
So on the merits, it doesn't make sense.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:21.030
And also in terms of what it signals in terms
of the, the sort of ambience or atmosphere

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.940
that's such a policy creates.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.020
It's not doing anything for us and it's actually
doing a lot against us.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:27.630
So Bernie would stop that.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:33.150
Then we get into some of the key elements
of the immigration debate, one of which is

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:38.949
DACA deferred action for childhood arrivals,
child arrivals, meaning individuals who even

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:46.449
if they are older than 18 today, 18 or older
today, they came to the United States as miners

00:04:46.449 --> 00:04:48.189
brought by their parents.

00:04:48.189 --> 00:04:53.020
And it doesn't make sense to punish people
for circumstances that are not of their doing.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:58.520
Bernie would extend DACA to the 1.8 million
who are currently eligible for it and also

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:03.660
allow those who have been in the United States
undocumented for more than five years to remain

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.330
without threat of deportation.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:11.460
And as we know, if you are here, even if you're
not a committing criminal acts as almost all

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:17.389
undocumented immigrants are not doing, and
all of those, uh, circumstances are true.

00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:23.189
If you know you face deportation, you are
not going to be contributing to society.

00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:27.520
And the way that you otherwise would be able
to and would choose to because that fear of

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.650
deportation would keep you hidden.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:33.919
It would keep you in circumstances where you're
not a productive member of society.

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:34.919
That's a good thing.

00:05:34.919 --> 00:05:36.599
But then what's the bigger picture of that?

00:05:36.599 --> 00:05:38.259
The path to citizenship?

00:05:38.259 --> 00:05:41.919
Bernie would, and I liked that he's being
upfront about this piece.

00:05:41.919 --> 00:05:47.680
Bernie would push Congress for a path to citizenship
for the 11 million who are already here in

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.069
the United States, undocumented living and
working today.

00:05:51.069 --> 00:05:54.740
And Bernie's being upfront, he's not saying
I will do this because he can't do this.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.580
It is something he needs to push Congress
to do and he's being clear about that in the

00:05:58.580 --> 00:05:59.580
plan.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:06.099
A importantly, things like marijuana possession
and other, uh, um, uh, you know, nonviolent

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:09.710
of violations of the law will not block the
path to citizenship.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:11.439
That makes absolute and total sense.

00:06:11.439 --> 00:06:13.749
And I like that that is included here.

00:06:13.749 --> 00:06:21.279
A Bernie would also repeal, uh, the eight
us code section 13, 25, which puts border

00:06:21.279 --> 00:06:26.050
crossings on par with other types of immigration
violations, like overstaying a visa.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:32.740
It would not, it would change the circumstances
where a border crossing has some higher criminal

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:36.069
consideration that simply overstaying a visa
does not.

00:06:36.069 --> 00:06:38.180
It would put it all on par.

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:43.059
Now as far as immigration agencies, Bernie
wants to break up ice and customs and border

00:06:43.059 --> 00:06:45.830
patrol, but not eliminate the function.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:46.830
Simply redistribute them.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:51.399
And this is an important thing because on
the one hand you have ice and CBP which are,

00:06:51.399 --> 00:06:57.280
uh, extraordinarily mismanaged, inefficient
and involved in a lot of things that we want

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:58.280
to curtail.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:04.849
But at the same time, if you simply say abolish
ice and CBP and eliminate their functions

00:07:04.849 --> 00:07:10.710
altogether, you will get of large contingent
of the American public saying that is a responsible.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:17.099
So what Bernie would do is he would bring
back the enforcement of deportations and of

00:07:17.099 --> 00:07:20.479
a border investigative authority to the department
of justice.

00:07:20.479 --> 00:07:26.139
Doesn't mean we eliminated, we simply move
it from ice and CBP back to DOJ.

00:07:26.139 --> 00:07:31.379
Customs authority would go from CBP back to
the treasury department the way it used to

00:07:31.379 --> 00:07:37.179
be and the naturalization and citizenship
authority would be given back to the state

00:07:37.179 --> 00:07:38.179
department.

00:07:38.179 --> 00:07:42.449
Makes absolute and total sense now is when
we start getting into the really interesting

00:07:42.449 --> 00:07:43.479
stuff.

00:07:43.479 --> 00:07:48.949
You know, that this is a thoughtful and well-crafted
plan because Bernie is including elements

00:07:48.949 --> 00:07:55.279
like strengthening immigrant labor rights
in immigration reform, which have a place

00:07:55.279 --> 00:07:57.449
there that's exactly what we should be doing.

00:07:57.449 --> 00:08:03.789
So for example, there will be a whistle blower
visa established to protect immigrant workers

00:08:03.789 --> 00:08:07.289
who are reporting exploited of actions by
employers.

00:08:07.289 --> 00:08:13.029
One of the ways in which big corporations
remain the major beneficiaries of undocumented

00:08:13.029 --> 00:08:19.669
immigration is that they have, um, of of all,
almost carte blanche to have horrible working

00:08:19.669 --> 00:08:25.089
conditions, uh, because they know that they're
undocumented workers that they are employing,

00:08:25.089 --> 00:08:26.460
which by the way is against the law.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:28.099
But good luck holding them accountable.

00:08:28.099 --> 00:08:29.150
We're not doing it.

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:33.849
Um, they know that they are not in a position
to go and report those conditions for fear

00:08:33.849 --> 00:08:34.849
of deportation.

00:08:34.849 --> 00:08:39.910
A whistleblower visa would protect them, restructuring
work visas to make them portable.

00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:45.640
Because when you have a work visa that is
tied to one employer, only that employee is

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.560
trapped at that employer.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:51.090
And again, it diminishes, diminishes their
labor rights and labor power.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:52.530
Very great.

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.280
Absolutely fantastic thing to do.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:59.240
Ending workplace raids and shifting the focus
of the employment of the enforcement from

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:00.700
the workers to the employers.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:02.760
I've been talking about this for years.

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:05.150
You've got to deal with the employers here.

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:06.450
Bernie would do that.

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:07.540
Protecting farm workers.

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:09.170
There's lots of ways that Bernie does.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:10.170
This.

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:14.550
One is requiring at least a $15 minimum wage
and overtime pay for agricultural workers.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:19.370
They, uh, there are all these exemptions for
agricultural workers from a lot of the labor

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:21.320
laws that protect other workers.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:23.200
You've got to get rid of those loopholes.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:24.200
Medicare for all.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:25.200
Now you might say, wait a second.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:28.100
What does Medicare for all have to do with
immigration?

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:29.190
Everything.

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:30.280
Everything.

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:35.540
When you look at the disparities in health
care, along racial lines and among immigrant

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:42.660
versus domestically born, uh, if you are going
to comprehensively do immigration reform,

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:47.410
you need to deal with, uh, the system that
we have, which doesn't work and Medicare for

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:49.530
all is Bernie's way of dealing with it.

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:54.190
Bernie also says that he will use executive
orders whenever possible.

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:57.110
Now, I think that this is both smart and honest.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:10:01.800
Bernie is saying, uh, I can't use executive
orders for all of these things.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.340
Bernie is not pretending that he can just
snap his finger and create a path to citizenship

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:07.770
for undocumented immigrants.

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:12.390
Even if that's what he would like to do on
that issue, he will push Congress to do it

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:18.430
where he can use executive orders, he will
use them and he will also work to undo the

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:19.430
work.

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:24.370
Uh, the disastrous work that Donald Trump
has done on this issue via executive orders.

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:29.250
There's a lot more and sub points to many
of these, but these are the primary things.

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:32.990
So question one, now Ken, Bernie just do this
stuff.

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:35.750
If he becomes president, the answer is no,
of course not.

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:39.060
And he's being upfront about the elements
that he can't do by himself.

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:40.060
But that's okay.

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:43.380
A campaign policy platform is merely the starting
point.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:48.960
It is what the candidate would like, but let's
at least start from the right place, right?

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:53.150
We don't start healthcare negate what we did,
uh, under a former president.

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:58.450
But we shouldn't start healthcare negotiations
under we, we would, we would like a public

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:02.000
option if, if it's okay, but if not, we'll
just do Obamacare.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:08.220
No, we start the negotiations from the morally
and ethically correct place that is in line

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:10.920
with our values and what we would like to
see.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:12.600
And you negotiate from there.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:18.820
So here's how I would sum up what our immigration
policy should be as a principal or as a value

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:22.760
for starting the negotiation and the right
claims to love this stuff.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:25.770
The right doesn't really want to do the work
of the nitty gritty.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:30.180
The right loves to say, here are our values,
here are our principles.

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:32.370
These will guide us and give us all of the
answers.

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:35.490
They don't, by the way, but let's do it.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:40.560
Here's the principle from which our policies
on immigration should follow.

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:44.010
Let's treat people like human beings.

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.210
Let's treat human beings like people.

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:50.490
Let's not punish people for circumstances
beyond their control.

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:55.350
And let's live up to the standards that make
the United States what it is or at least what

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:57.260
it is supposed to be, period.

00:11:57.260 --> 00:11:58.260
Okay.

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:04.000
All of the correct policies follow from that
if you apply it responsibly and if you apply

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:08.910
it in a thoughtful way to all elements of
the immigration situation and as Bernie Sanders

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:13.790
plan points out, that includes healthcare,
that includes labor rights, that includes

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:20.510
actually buckling down on employers who are
breaking the law yet go virtually unpunished.

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:23.990
So a really good plan from Bernie Sanders
on this issue.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:28.570
If there are things missing from it that you
would like to see, let me know.

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:32.301
I'll give you one example of something I would've
liked to see more of.

00:12:32.301 --> 00:12:40.520
One of the most important factors in influencing
a, the number of people that want to come

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:46.550
to the United States legally or illegally
are the circumstances in their home country

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:48.330
that they are coming from.

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:53.450
And so one of the additional things that I
would love to see more of in a serious and

00:12:53.450 --> 00:13:01.150
comprehensive immigration plan is ideas or
policies for how we can modify our relationship

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:02.820
or improved circumstances.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:07.230
In some of the source countries such that
our relationships with those countries will

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:08.750
imp improve economically.

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:14.390
We'll be, we will be better off by virtue
of helping their economies and we will just

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:18.640
reduce the demand for the number of people
that are trying to come over to the United

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:21.120
States who are willing to do so illegally.

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:24.500
That is what I would like to see more of in
the plan, but I want to hear from you as well.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:26.680
I'm on Twitter at D Pacman.

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:30.160
The show is on Twitter at David Pakman show
